verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =2\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "27044530c516a3e55c2b08d31c2f639b85779eaec00b6f81488051ef85f5a4dd" "basis" 1248 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2affa56a7b3908a997343832a437005a4cc286057c7e6dc1be2001b32bcc917" "basis" 1248 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/math