urse_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/state.json" "file_sha256" "b368523ad4eef2f43817e1a8216e534017e2c2976c9deee9b346c75d12ec2bd8"} {"question" "q-117457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "775bf27c177020e55c870f78c963c0a325aada640a0b6dc177cb00c1f01938b8"} {"question" "q-117457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/state.json" "file_sha256" "b368523ad4eef2f43817e1a8216e534017e2c2976c9deee9b346c75d12ec2bd8"} {"question" "q-117457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/state.json" "file_sha256" "b368523ad4eef2f43817e1a8216e534017e2c2976c9deee9b346c75d12ec2bd8"} {"question" "q-117457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/state.json" "file_sha256" "b368523ad4eef2f43817e1a8216e534017e2c2976c9deee9b346c75d12ec2bd8"} {"question" "e-6754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/example-6754.json" "file_sha256" "7e5bb522d3e2fb9f0e3148246d391477fa96c2cb915ce1188156e648d054d6c4"} {"question" "e-6754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The range of $\\theta = \\arccos x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta  \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(  -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/example-6754.json" "file_sha256" "7e5bb522d3e2fb9f0e3148246d391477fa96c2cb915ce1188156e648d054d6c4"} {"question" "e-7220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/example-7220.json" "file_sha256" "9e9cf2ba68998f473cc34c5880bd94365b30b458dc338e991da63ebc0726e9fe"} {"question" "e-7220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arcsin x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta.$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ and lies within the range of $\\theta,$ we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/example-7220.json" "file_sha256" "9e9cf2ba68998f473cc34c5880bd94365b30b458dc338e991da63ebc0726e9fe"} {"question" "e-6755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/example-6755.json" "file_sha256" "3c50abe2033c890d9e1e47bf591ecbd8a1fef70fbdc84a806476fabefa01771c"} {"question" "e-6755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arctan x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/example-6755.json" "file_sha256" "3c50abe2033c890d9e1e47bf591ecbd8a1fef70fbdc84a806476fabefa01771c"}] "usage" {"e-6755" [] "q-117412" [] "q-320893" [] "q-320721" [] "q-24758" [] "e-7220" [] "q-320665" [] "e-6754" [] "q-24756" [] "q-117462" [] "q-117408" []} "basis" 1257 "retractions" [[17592186295381 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={30}^{∘}.\n$$"] [17592186259568 :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$"] [17592186259568 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"] [17592186278569 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978511/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295360 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =2\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186269024 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =4\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={-180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060166 :question/problem "What is $\\arctan (\\sqrt{3})$?"] [17592186059533 :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta = \\arccos x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta  \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(  -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186059544 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arcsin x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta.$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ and lies within the range of $\\theta,$ we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186057995 17592186057996 17592186057997 17592186057998 17592186057999 17592186259570 17592186269006 17592186269007 17592186269008 17592186269009 17592186269010 17592186269023 17592186269026 17592186269027 17592186278571 17592186278572 17592186278573 17592186278574 17592186278575 17592186295362 17592186295363 17592186295364 17592186295365 17592186295366 17592186295383 17592186295384 17592186295385 17592186295386 17592186295387] "immutable_fields" [[17592186057994 :answer-field/correct] [17592186057994 :answer-field/id] [17592186057994 :answer-field/key] [17592186057994 :answer-field/type] [17592186259569 :answer-field/correct] [17592186259569 :answer-field/id] [17592186259569 :answer-field/key] [17592186259569 :answer-field/type] [17592186269005 :answer-field/correct] [17592186269005 :answer-field/id] [17592186269005 :answer-field/key] [17592186269005 :answer-field/type] [17592186269022 :answer-field/correct] [17592186269022 :answer-field/id] [17592186269022 :answer-field/key] [17592186269022 :answer-field/type] [17592186269025 :answer-field/correct] [17592186269025 :answer-field/id] [17592186269025 :answer-field/key] [17592186269025 :answer-field/type] [17592186278570 :answer-field/correct] [17592186278570 :answer-field/id] [17592186278570 :answer-field/key] [17592186278570 :answer-field/type] [17592186295361 :answer-field/correct] [17592186295361 :answer-field/id] [17592186295361 :answer-field/key] [17592186295361 :answer-field/type] [17592186295382 :answer-field/correct] [17592186295382 :answer-field/id] [17592186295382 :answer-field/key] [17592186295382 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
